 ### [View-Dependent Appearance Representations](14602-view-dependent-appearance-representations.md)
-This family of methods extends the standard 3D Gaussian primitive with appearance attributes $\mathbf{a}_i$ to model view-dependent color $c_i(\mathbf{v}, \mathbf{a}_i)$, where $\mathbf{v}$ is the viewing direction. Unlike static color storage, these representations decode color dynamically based on the viewing angle, enabling the modeling of specular highlights, anisotropy, and complex lighting effects. The rendering pipeline retains front-to-back alpha-blending, but the color contribution of each Gaussian is computed via an appearance function that queries $\mathbf{a}_i$ using $\mathbf{v}$, surface normal $\mathbf{n}$, or local coordinates. This approach trades off storage bandwidth, decoding computational cost, and representation complexity. See [View-Dependent Appearance Representations](14602-view-dependent-appearance-representations.md).
