 ## Background
-The standard 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) method originally used Spherical Harmonics (SH) to represent view-dependent color [S1]. However, SH has limitations in capturing high-frequency details and complex lighting effects [S1]. Recent advances introduced explicit 2D textures and neural appearance models to overcome these limitations [S1][S2, unofficial]. View-Dependent Gaussian Splatting (VDGS) proposed using NeRF-like networks to predict view-dependent attributes [S1]. Neural Texture Splatting extended this by encoding textures as neural networks [S2, unofficial]. These methods aim to improve visual fidelity while maintaining the real-time performance of Gaussian splatting [S1][S2, unofficial].
